avformat/hls: start a VOD playlist at its #EXT-X-START

A transcode started at an offset (Plex's start offset) lists the whole
film, with #EXT-X-START:TIME-OFFSET at the offset, and answers every
segment before it nearly empty (188 bytes of media, a bare WEBVTT
header). The demuxer started a VOD playlist at its first segment, so:
- the open read through hundreds of empty segments, one request each,
  before the first frame (about 5 s at an offset of 600 s on Plex, 26 s
  at 3600 s);
- the stream's start was the offset's packet time, so a player's
  position began at 0 there and its seeks, counted from that start,
  landed that many segments early.

RFC 8216 4.4.2.2 says a client SHOULD start at that offset. A VOD
playlist now starts at the segment holding it on its time line (a
negative offset counts from the end; one past either end means that
end). A rendition that names none takes the first offset another
finished playlist names, as they share the time line, else the
master's. The start is still the playlist's origin (the first packet's
time less the segments skipped, as the existing first_read_seq_no
rebase does), so a position is the film's and seeks map right, before
the offset too. PRECISE=YES is not taken: the start is the segment's,
as with PRECISE=NO; a caller's own start, a seek, is exact.

A master may name #EXT-X-START before its variants. The tag made the
master a media playlist (ensure_playlist()), and its first
#EXT-X-STREAM-INF then failed the open ("Master Playlist tag found in a
Media Playlist"), in stock FFmpeg too. The offset is now kept and placed
at the end of the parse, on the media playlist or as the master's.

vod_x_start (default 1) turns the start off; a player probes it. A live
playlist keeps its own start rules.

Our own patch.

